<h2>What's actually in EXIF data</h2>
<p>
  EXIF (Exchangeable Image File Format) is a metadata standard embedded directly in JPG and TIFF files (PNG uses a related but different metadata mechanism) by the camera or phone that captured the image. A typical smartphone photo's EXIF data can include:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>GPS coordinates</strong> — the exact latitude and longitude where the photo was taken, down to a precision of a few meters, if location services were enabled.</li>
  <li><strong>Date and time</strong> — down to the second, including timezone in many cases.</li>
  <li><strong>Device information</strong> — the exact camera or phone model, sometimes down to the specific lens used.</li>
  <li><strong>Camera settings</strong> — aperture, shutter speed, ISO, focal length — useful to photographers reviewing their own shot history, largely irrelevant to anyone else.</li>
  <li><strong>Orientation flag</strong> — which way the image should be displayed, which is why removing all metadata (rather than a selective strip) occasionally requires re-baking orientation into the pixel data so photos don't display sideways afterward.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why this matters for privacy</h2>
<p>
  GPS coordinates are the specific concern. A photo taken inside your home, at your child's school, or at a location you'd rather not disclose — shared with its original EXIF data intact — reveals the exact coordinates of that location to anyone who checks. This isn't hypothetical: journalists, researchers, and security professionals have repeatedly demonstrated locating people from EXIF data in photos shared through channels that don't strip it (personal blogs, direct messaging apps, email).
</p>
<p>
  The practical risk depends entirely on where the photo is going. Uploading to Instagram or Facebook: the platform strips this data during processing, so it's not a live concern on the platform itself (though the platform itself now has the data). Emailing a full-resolution photo directly, posting it to a personal website, or sending it through a messaging app that preserves originals: the EXIF data travels intact unless you strip it first.
</p>

<h2>When stripping metadata matters most</h2>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Real estate photos</strong> shared before a listing goes live, where the address shouldn't be inferable ahead of the public listing.</li>
  <li><strong>Photos of children</strong> shared publicly, where a location trail isn't something most parents want attached.</li>
  <li><strong>Personal or sensitive locations</strong> — home, workplace, a private event — in photos posted anywhere the audience isn't fully trusted.</li>
  <li><strong>Professional submissions</strong> where camera/device metadata isn't relevant and cleaner files are simply more professional to submit.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Privacy: what happens to your photos</h2>
<p>
  Metadata stripping happens entirely in your browser — photos are never uploaded to check or remove their EXIF data, which matters more than usual for a privacy-focused tool: sending a photo to a server specifically to strip its location data would mean trusting that server with the very location data you're trying to protect.
</p>

